1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,180
Close your eyes. Write the name.

2
00:00:04,620 --> 00:00:09,400
Say it in your head. You know it the way
you write your own address.

3
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:12,120
The way you write your own name.

4
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:19,460
Yet you're wrong. The publisher's extensive
records disagree with what you think you

5
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:27,400
know. The Berenstain Bears. The

6
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:32,140
series debuted in 1962 with a book called
The Big Honey Hunt,

7
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:37,260
published by Random House, authored by
Stanley and Janice Berenstain.

8
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:40,840
The name has been spelled that way on
every cover,

9
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:47,160
every spine, every television adaptation,
every author's byline since the first printing.

10
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:50,360
There is no addition that uses EIN.

11
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:52,820
There is no alternate version.

12
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:55,100
There is no other publisher.

13
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,160
The record is unambiguous.

14
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:00,780
And yet millions of adults.

15
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:04,780
Not a few. Not an odd pocket of confused
readers.

16
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:08,320
But millions. Remember it the other way.

17
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:15,800
They remember it with the certainty of
muscle memory.

18
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:19,720
The way you remember a phone number you've
dialed a thousand times.

19
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:23,680
The certainty that you know before you
check.

20
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:27,460
Cognitive scientists have a clean
explanation.

21
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:34,540
German -derived surnames ending in Stien
are statistically far more common than those

22
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:38,380
ending in Stain. The brain, during
encoding,

23
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:42,020
substitutes the familiar pattern for the
unfamiliar one.

24
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:46,320
Memory is reconstructive, not archival.

25
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:49,620
The mind fills gaps with what belongs
there,

26
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:51,720
not with what was actually there.

27
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:54,300
The explanation is sound.

28
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:59,160
It has been replicated. It is by every
measurable standard.

29
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:04,040
Correct. It explains why you remember it
wrong.

30
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:06,740
It does not explain when.

31
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:12,900
On July 4th 2012, CERN held a press
conference in Geneva,

32
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,380
Switzerland. The announcement was the
Higgs boson.

33
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:21,540
The particle physicists had been searching
for since it was predicted in 1964.

34
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:24,620
The date was not accidental.

35
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:28,040
July 4th has international resonance.

36
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:31,720
It generates press attention on multiple
continents.

37
00:02:32,460 --> 00:02:35,560
The announcement would be seen by the
largest possible audience.

38
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:38,240
A public relations decision.

39
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,100
Neither accounts for the pattern
underneath both.

40
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:46,000
September 29th, 1954.

41
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:48,460
Geneva, Switzerland.

42
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:52,220
The European Organization for Nuclear
Research,

43
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:58,100
CERN, from its French name, Conseil
Europeen pour la Recherche Nucleaire,

44
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,380
was formally established. 12 founding
nations.

45
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:06,720
A mandate to investigate the fundamental
structure of matter.

46
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:12,480
A site in Meron, near Geneva, straddling
the Franco -Swiss border.

47
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:18,960
The large Hadron Collider took 10 years and
approximately 10 billion dollars to build.

48
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:23,040
The ring is 27 kilometers in
circumference,

49
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:26,720
buried approximately 100 meters below
ground.

50
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:30,080
The tunnel follows the curvature of the
earth.

51
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:34,240
The Franco -Swiss border running through
it like a seam.

52
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:37,960
Construction began in 1998.

53
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:41,960
The last connections were made in 2008.

54
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:48,320
On September 10th 2008, at 10.28 in the
morning,

55
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:55,500
a proton beam was successfully steered around
the full 27 kilometer ring for the first

56
00:03:55,500 --> 00:04:00,120
time. Amara Fabiola was on shift when it
happened.

57
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:07,280
Not in the main control room, where the
room of engineers and physicists had spent a

58
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:12,780
decade building towards this moment, watching
a point of light complete its first full

59
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:14,700
circuit on the monitoring screens.

60
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:18,940
Fabiola was in the calibration monitoring
room.

61
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,620
A smaller space, adjacent.

62
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:27,760
Three rows of screens showing data that most
people in the building had stopped consciously

63
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:32,440
reading years ago. The background, the
substrate,

64
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:37,200
the electromagnetic field that all the
measurement instruments swam in,

65
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:40,680
which had to remain stable for the
measurements to mean anything.

66
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:45,840
The main room erupted when the beam
completed its first circuit.

67
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:48,840
Fabiola heard it through the wall.

68
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:55,260
A sound that started as noise and resolved
into something more like recognition.

69
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:03,140
600 engineers and physicists simultaneously
understanding that the decade of

70
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:05,820
work had produced what it was supposed to
produce.

71
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:08,960
Fabiola looked at the screens.

72
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:13,860
The readings were doing something small,
not alarming.

73
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:20,380
Calibration work is a discipline built on
the understanding that small is not the same

74
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:26,900
as nothing. The ambient field in Sector 34
was showing a resonance signature that

75
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:29,940
Fabiola had not seen in the pre -run
baseline data.

76
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:34,480
A frequency. The instruments were
reporting it clearly,

77
00:05:35,240 --> 00:05:37,420
approximately 7.9 hertz.

78
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:41,260
Duration, 0.8 seconds.

79
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:47,240
Then gone. The celebrations continued
through the wall.

80
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:51,940
Fabiola noted the reading in the
operational log,

81
00:05:52,620 --> 00:05:56,100
tagged it for follow -up, and went back to
watching the screens.

82
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:58,760
The beam had run.

83
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:03,860
The machine worked. The 7.9 hertz anomaly
was small.

84
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:10,000
It was the kind of small thing a calibration
engineer notes and recommends investigating,

85
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:16,480
and then the helium explodes, and the note
sits in a batch of pre -incident documentation

86
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:21,300
for 11 years until a partial FOIA request
surfaces it,

87
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:26,380
and then it sits in a different place and
waits for someone to know why it

88
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:33,280
matters. It matters because of what
happened in Colorado Springs in 1899.

89
00:06:34,900 --> 00:06:41,800
Nine days later, September 19th, 2008, a
faulty electrical

90
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:47,680
connection between two superconducting magnets
caused approximately six tons of liquid

91
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,380
helium to explosively vent into the
tunnel.

92
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:53,180
The sound was heard on the surface.

93
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:56,160
53 magnets were damaged.

94
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:58,760
The LHC went dark.

95
00:06:59,530 --> 00:07:01,580
The repairs would take over a year.

96
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:07,060
Nine minutes before the explosion, at 11
.47 in the morning,

97
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:13,000
an instrumentation monitoring memo was
logged in Sector 3 -4 of the LHC.

98
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:17,820
The memo was stamped Restricted, Internal
Use Only.

99
00:07:18,780 --> 00:07:26,100
It was filed by Department Code IM -34,
one of the standard calibration subgroups

100
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:30,840
responsible for monitoring the LHC's
background electromagnetic environment.

101
00:07:31,780 --> 00:07:34,740
Not the beam, not the particle
interactions,

102
00:07:35,180 --> 00:07:39,400
but the ambient electromagnetic field that
all the instruments swim in,

103
00:07:40,040 --> 00:07:42,900
which must be stable for the measurements
to mean anything.

104
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:47,720
The memo noted that during the September
10th first beam run,

105
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:53,880
instruments in Sector 3 -4 had recorded a
resonance signature in the background field,

106
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:58,180
not in the beam, not in any particle
interaction,

107
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:01,580
in the ambient environment surrounding the
ring.

108
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:05,400
The signature lasted 0.8 seconds.

109
00:08:06,220 --> 00:08:09,540
It corresponded to no -known particle
interaction pattern.

110
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:13,660
The frequency logged was approximately 7
.9 hertz.

111
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:20,220
The memo flagged it for follow -up and
recommended additional calibration before run

112
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:23,380
2. The follow -up was never filed.

113
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:29,400
The helium explosion made the September 10th
data irrelevant to the incident investigation.

114
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:35,840
The memo was included in a batch of pre
-incident operational documentation released

115
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:39,600
in a 2019 partial FOIA request.

116
00:08:40,280 --> 00:08:43,980
Not highlighted, not flagged, just there.

117
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:50,420
Logged at 1147, 0.8 seconds, 7.9 hertz.

118
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,060
Filed and not followed up.

119
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:59,780
What does 7.9 hertz mean to a CERN
calibration engineer in 2008?

120
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:02,780
It means an instrument anomaly.

121
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:06,760
It means a calibration task before the
next run.

122
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:11,540
The frequency sits within the range of
natural electromagnetic phenomena.

123
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:18,020
It is not alarming, it is procedurally
thorough to note it and recommend follow -up.

124
00:09:18,740 --> 00:09:24,880
What does 7.9 hertz mean to someone who
knows what happened in Colorado Springs in

125
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:28,580
1899? Something else entirely.

126
00:09:30,580 --> 00:09:32,220
May 1899.

127
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:34,720
Colorado Springs, Colorado.

128
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:37,220
Altitude 6000 feet.

129
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:40,120
The air thinner than it should be.

130
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:43,700
The kind of thinness the body knows
without being told.

131
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:47,240
A slight compression at the top of each
breath.

132
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:50,660
As though the atmosphere has been diluted,

133
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:55,380
requiring a slightly more deliberate
breath than lower ground demands.

134
00:09:56,560 --> 00:10:02,420
Nikola Tesla arrived in May of 1899 with a
specific purpose.

135
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:07,080
He had leased a large plot on the eastern
edge of the city.

136
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:13,360
Distant enough from the existing electrical
infrastructure that the experiments he planned

137
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:15,360
would not be constrained by it.

138
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:19,060
And would not, he hoped, destroy it.

139
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:22,900
He built what he called a magnifying
transmitter.

140
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:26,000
A Tesla coil of extraordinary scale.

141
00:10:26,820 --> 00:10:29,220
The primary coil 9 meters in diameter.

142
00:10:30,140 --> 00:10:34,880
The secondary coil wound to resonate at a
frequency he had spent years calculating.

143
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:38,640
The barn that housed it was built for the
purpose.

144
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:40,920
The equipment filled it.

145
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:45,200
When the transmitter ran at anything
approaching full power,

146
00:10:45,960 --> 00:10:47,980
the air inside the barn was different.

147
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:52,780
Not glowing. Not producing visible arcs at
low power.

148
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:58,940
But pressurized. The way air behaves in
the seconds before a lightning strike,

149
00:10:59,620 --> 00:11:01,200
when the channel has been selected.

150
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:04,120
But the discharge hasn't travelled it yet.

151
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:07,240
Charged. Waiting.

152
00:11:08,780 --> 00:11:13,420
The smell inside the barn was copper wire
under load and insulation warming past its

153
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:16,740
rating. The specific smell of electrical
stress.

154
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:20,160
Acrid and sharp. And beneath that.

155
00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:24,240
Ozone. The byproduct of arc discharge.

156
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:27,380
The smell of air being split by current.

157
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:30,440
The smell of a specific frequency of
energy.

158
00:11:30,940 --> 00:11:34,920
Doing something to the atmosphere that the
atmosphere did not do on its own.

159
00:11:36,140 --> 00:11:38,320
Tesla was not a small man.

160
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:41,360
Six feet two. Lean.

161
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:45,720
With a kind of physical economy that
suggested nothing was wasted.

162
00:11:46,580 --> 00:11:49,520
He understood, from years of
experimentation,

163
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:55,140
exactly what would happen when the
transmitter reached its operating parameters.

164
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:57,560
He had calculated it.

165
00:11:58,360 --> 00:12:03,980
He had modeled it. But calculation and
experience are different things.

166
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:09,460
And what happened on the night the
magnifying transmitter ran at full power,

167
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:12,420
was not available to calculation alone.

168
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:17,340
The lightning bolt arced 135 feet.

169
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:21,180
Outward and downward simultaneously.

170
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:29,100
Branching. The branching following the
path of

171
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:32,600
lit like a road being shown to something
enormous.

172
00:12:33,620 --> 00:12:35,900
The thunder did not arrive after the
light.

173
00:12:36,540 --> 00:12:38,280
The sound was not sound.

174
00:12:39,100 --> 00:12:43,920
It was pressure. It hit the ribs before
the ears processed it.

175
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:48,340
The air displaced faster than the body
could brace.

176
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:54,220
And Tesla was standing close enough that the
shockwave pushed at the edges of his vision.

177
00:12:55,080 --> 00:13:01,580
The periphery going soft and white for half
a second before the world reasserted itself.

178
00:13:02,740 --> 00:13:05,020
Then the city went dark.

179
00:13:06,220 --> 00:13:07,800
Not a gradual dimming.

180
00:13:08,880 --> 00:13:13,800
A cut. The hum of Colorado springs's
electrical infrastructure.

181
00:13:14,700 --> 00:13:18,100
The background frequency he had not
consciously registered for months.

182
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:21,740
The way you stop hearing a clock. Stopped.

183
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:25,800
The silence had the shape of the sound
that had been there before.

184
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:32,080
Specific. The generator at the El Paso
Electric Company had burned out.

185
00:13:32,940 --> 00:13:37,220
Tesla had drawn more current than the
city's entire grid could supply.

186
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:41,020
In that silence, before anyone spoke.

187
00:13:42,060 --> 00:13:44,900
Before any assessment was made of what had
just happened.

188
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:48,900
The battery powered instruments were still
running.

189
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:53,140
They were isolated from the grid he'd just
destroyed.

190
00:13:54,060 --> 00:13:59,840
And what they showed in the first seconds
before the city began to respond to its

191
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:04,940
own darkness. Was something the instruments
had not been designed to report.

192
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:09,660
And could not explain by any prior
observation in the literature.

193
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:12,940
The earth itself was resonating.

194
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:16,040
The signal had propagated through the
ground.

195
00:14:16,780 --> 00:14:18,500
The planet was conducting it.

196
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:23,560
The standing wave had formed in the
terrestrial electromagnetic substrate.

197
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:26,420
Not in the air. Not in wires.

198
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:30,540
Not in anything man -made. But in the
earth.

199
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:35,060
The planet was behaving like a bell that
had been struck.

200
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:38,140
Tesla stood in the dark barn.

201
00:14:39,140 --> 00:14:42,120
His ears were still ringing from the
pressure of the discharge.

202
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:46,160
The smell of ozone was so thick it had
texture.

203
00:14:47,060 --> 00:14:48,740
The air didn't just carry it.

204
00:14:49,560 --> 00:14:55,520
It was made of it. A sharp mineral
presence that coated the back of the throat

205
00:14:55,520 --> 00:15:01,760
and stayed. The instruments were showing him
something that should not have been possible.

206
00:15:02,620 --> 00:15:05,140
Not impossible in the scientific sense.

207
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:07,800
He had calculated this result.

208
00:15:08,760 --> 00:15:11,660
Had designed the experiment to produce
exactly this.

209
00:15:12,540 --> 00:15:14,740
Impossible in the experiential sense.

210
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:20,260
The gap between a calculation and the
moment you stand in the dark and watch the

211
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:23,300
calculation become true in an instrument
reading.

212
00:15:24,200 --> 00:15:26,180
The planet had answered.

213
00:15:27,820 --> 00:15:32,040
He held very still. His hands were at his
sides.

214
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:39,580
Not touching anything. The deliberate stillness
of a man who understood that any movement

215
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:44,420
toward the instruments was a movement that
might disturb what the instruments were showing.

216
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:47,720
The ozone was still thick in the air.

217
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:52,960
Coating the back of the throat with a
taste like the inside of a struck match.

218
00:15:54,160 --> 00:15:57,040
The battery -powered instruments ticked in
the dark.

219
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:00,260
The needles held their positions.

220
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:04,480
Outside, somewhere in the direction of the
city,

221
00:16:05,320 --> 00:16:09,320
there were voices. The distant human
response to sudden darkness.

222
00:16:10,380 --> 00:16:12,760
The sounds a population makes when the
lights cut.

223
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:16,620
And the night becomes immediately and
completely real.

224
00:16:17,440 --> 00:16:19,220
A dog. A shout.

225
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:23,940
The specific confusion of a grid going
down all at once.

226
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:28,980
The sudden silence of every electrical
machine in an eight -block radius.

227
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:34,360
The way that silence has a shape because
you only perceive it when it stops.

228
00:16:35,280 --> 00:16:37,940
Tesla did not move toward those sounds.

229
00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:40,040
He stayed with the instruments.

230
00:16:40,620 --> 00:16:45,580
He watched the reading. His left hand
found the edge of the instrument bench.

231
00:16:46,620 --> 00:16:49,020
And rested there without gripping it.

232
00:16:49,660 --> 00:16:53,300
He committed the frequency to the notes he
would write before morning.

233
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:56,100
Approximately eight hertz.

234
00:16:57,720 --> 00:17:00,180
The frequency at which the earth
resonates.

235
00:17:01,440 --> 00:17:07,740
The frequency Tesla had just discovered by
striking the planet like a bell and listening

236
00:17:07,740 --> 00:17:14,620
to what it said back. He described in letters
and notes from the same period receiving

237
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:20,000
signals during the Colorado Springs experiments
that he could not account for by any

238
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:25,780
terrestrial source. Signals that seemed to
arrive from outside the experimental system

